\DOC ADD_IBT_OPEN_EXISTS

\TYPE {ADD_IBT_OPEN_EXISTS : thm -> (thm -> tactic) -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Applied with a theorem and a tactic-producing continuation, this tactic closes
the current goal outright, leaving no subgoals.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument {th : thm} is a theorem; in the observed traces it is a
specification theorem whose statement closely parallels the goal, differing in
the code constant and side conditions. The second argument {k : thm -> tactic}
is a continuation applied to a theorem derived from {th}, supplying the tactic
that performs the remaining work; in the observed traces it is a large
composite tactic that specialises the theorem, rewrites with calling-convention
and MAYCHANGE abbreviations, and finishes the goal. Nothing of the conclusion
or the assumptions remains:
{
       A ?- t
       ==================  ADD_IBT_OPEN_EXISTS th k
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this closing behaviour, and only on goals with no
assumptions whose conclusions are universally quantified correctness statements
(sometimes under a leading existential) of the form "side conditions imply an
{ensures} property".

\ENDDOC
